- `action(n1, "start the process for WIGGO")`: Hallucinates a step; the source text uses this phrase purely as introductory context ("To begin the process for WIGGO, start by checking..."), not as a distinct executable task.
- `action(n4, "End")`: Hallucinates a step; the source indicates the process naturally terminates ("the process will come to an end") immediately after the final check, with no separate "End" action described.
- `precedence(i1, n1)` & `precedence(n1, n2)`: Violates source chronology; control flow should proceed directly from `init(i1)` to `action(n2, "check incoming order")` to faithfully mirror "start by checking the incoming order".
- `precedence(n3, n4)` & `precedence(n4, e1)`: Violates source chronology and intended progression; control flow should proceed directly from `action(n3, "check customer")` to `end(e1)` to faithfully mirror "Once the customer has been checked, the process will come to an end".